1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,575
You spent the whole night

2
00:00:01,615 --> 00:00:02,630
worrying about it.

3
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:04,603
And then… nothing happened.

4
00:00:05,053 --> 00:00:06,680
Psychologists at Penn State

5
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:07,915
had people who worry

6
00:00:07,955 --> 00:00:09,583
constantly write down their

7
00:00:09,623 --> 00:00:11,435
worries, four times a day, for

8
00:00:11,475 --> 00:00:13,164
ten days. Then, for a month

9
00:00:13,204 --> 00:00:13,967
they checked.

10
00:00:14,007 --> 00:00:15,530
Did they come true?

11
00:00:16,030 --> 00:00:18,033
About ninety-one percent...

12
00:00:18,073 --> 00:00:19,260
never happened.

13
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:20,731
For seven of them...

14
00:00:20,771 --> 00:00:22,774
not one single worry came

15
00:00:22,814 --> 00:00:23,573
true.

16
00:00:24,223 --> 00:00:25,846
So why does worry still feel

17
00:00:25,886 --> 00:00:27,450
so useful? Because when the

18
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:29,172
bad thing doesn't happen, you

19
00:00:29,212 --> 00:00:30,954
feel relief. That relief feels

20
00:00:30,994 --> 00:00:32,499
like proof. And your brain

21
00:00:32,539 --> 00:00:34,281
gives the credit to the worry.

22
00:00:34,321 --> 00:00:36,003
Like a rain dance, in a place

23
00:00:36,043 --> 00:00:37,700
where it rains anyway.

24
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:40,047
Try this. Write the worry down

25
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:41,824
as a prediction, with a date.

26
00:00:41,864 --> 00:00:43,071
Then check it.

27
00:00:43,571 --> 00:00:45,407
So… what did you worry about

28
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:46,813
last month that never

29
00:00:46,853 --> 00:00:47,806
happened?
